From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Playport <dev@playport.invalid>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 09:12:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: keep a WoW64 window until its threads and fault
 lookups are done

The owner's release (its initial thread's exit) unmapped the window
while secondary threads still ran on their TEBs and 32-bit stacks in it
(the server ends them only when it closes their pipes), and the Mach
handler's lock-free lookup could use a base after its window was gone
or its slot reused. A thread's pair and 32-bit stack also stayed in the
window until then, so a guest that created and joined workers ran out
of guest space after a few hundred.

wow64_threads.h now keeps a record per secondary thread. Release only
retires the window while any is left; a thread's record is marked
exited at the end of pthread_exit_wrapper, and a later exiting thread,
a thread creation, a reservation or a release joins it (one critical
section claims earlier exits and marks the caller's, so joins form no
cycle). virtual_free_thread_data then frees its 32-bit stack through
the window and its native stack, and keeps its TEB pair as a spare for
the owner's next thread. The retired window's last reclaimed thread
tears it down. exit_thread no longer joins a window thread as its
previous thread.

The Mach handler counts itself in (ios_wow64_fault_enter) before it
reads a window's owner and out after its emulated access; teardown
clears the owner and waits for that count to drain before the views go
or the slot is reused. Teardown also clears the dead threads' TEBs from
the thread registry, and NtTerminateThread's registry scan reads a TEB
with mach_vm_read, so neither dereferences a gone TEB.

Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-04-portal2-d3d9-vulkan.md
Offered-upstream: no; the window exists only on the iOS port
